@startuml cqrs-sequence
'==============================================================================
' Figure (Chapter III): the CQRS + Event Sourcing flow of cqrs-es.puml seen in
' time rather than in structure. Architecture level: same participants and same
' vocabulary as cqrs-es.puml, with NO routes, status codes or concrete
' command/event names -- those belong to the realisation chapter, whose
' backend-paths.puml carries the realised command and query paths.
' The point of this figure is the ORDERING: the caller is answered while the
' command path is still the only thing that has run, and the read side catches
' up afterwards. That is what a component view cannot show.
'
' SHAPE: a sequence diagram is scaled to \textwidth, so its ASPECT RATIO decides
' how much page it eats -- a narrow diagram renders tall. Keep the participant
' labels long (they also match cqrs-es.puml) and the message count low; do not
' "simplify" either without re-checking the rendered height.
' Render:  plantuml -tpdf cqrs-sequence.puml      (or -tpng)
'==============================================================================
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam defaultFontSize 20
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

' Spread the lifelines so the diagram is wide rather than tall (see SHAPE
' above). This MUST be the CSS style form: `skinparam ParticipantPadding` is
' deprecated in current PlantUML and prints a warning banner into the rendered
' figure, which then ships in the report.
<style>
sequenceDiagram {
  participant {
    Padding 30
  }
}
</style>

actor       "Client"                             as client
participant "Command bus"                        as cmd
participant "Aggregate\n(validates business rules)" as agg
database    "Event store\n(append-only)"         as es
queue       "Message broker"                     as broker
participant "Projections\n(read models)"         as proj
participant "Reactors\n(side effects)"           as reactor

== Command path (synchronous) ==
client -> cmd  : command
activate cmd
cmd -> agg     : validate and apply
activate agg
agg -> es      : append event
deactivate agg
cmd --> client : acknowledged
deactivate cmd

== Reactions (asynchronous) ==
es -> broker      : publish event
broker -> proj    : update read models
broker -> reactor : trigger
activate reactor
reactor -> cmd    : follow-up command
deactivate reactor

note over proj, reactor
  The client already holds its answer;
  the read side catches up here.
end note
@enduml
